## Statement

Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K:S^1\to S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot with a chosen product tubular neighbourhood $N(K)$, and set $E_K=S^3\setminus\operatorname{int}N(K)$ and $T_K=\partial E_K$. If $K$ is not ambient isotopic through orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms to the standard unknot, then every smoothly embedded $2$-sphere in $\operatorname{int}E_K$ bounds an embedded $3$-ball in $E_K$, and there is no smoothly embedded disk $D\subset E_K$ whose interior misses $T_K$ and whose boundary is an essential simple closed curve in $T_K$.

## Statement references

[Allen Hatcher, *Notes on Basic 3-Manifold Topology*, Theorem 1.1](https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/3M/3M.pdf)

[Stefan Friedl, *Introduction to Knots and Links*, Proposition 11.7](https://friedl.app.uni-regensburg.de/papers/2024_knot-theory-final.pdf)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** Full AC, a smooth oriented knot $K$, a chosen product tube $N(K)$, and the non-unknot hypothesis.

[A1] Full choice is the Axiom of Choice in [[def-axiom-of-choice]].

[L1] Full AC implies countable choice: for a sequence of nonempty sets $(X_n)$, apply a choice function to the set $\{X_n:n\in\mathbb N\}$ and evaluate it at $X_n$. This supplies the countable-choice hypothesis used below. [[def-countable-choice]]

[F1] [[def-knot-exterior]] defines $E_K=S^3\setminus\operatorname{int}N(K)$ and $T_K=\partial E_K$ for a chosen product tube.

[F2] [[def-knot-meridian-and-preferred-longitude]] defines the positive meridian as the oriented boundary of a normal disk and a preferred longitude as a degree-one boundary curve with zero image in $H_1(E_K;\mathbb Z)$.

[F3] [[lem-knot-exterior-has-peripheral-torus]] gives $T_K\cong T^2$ and its product meridian-section basis in $H_1(T_K;\mathbb Z)$.

[F4] [[thm-knot-exterior-homology-and-longitude]] assumes full AC and gives $H_1(E_K;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z[\mu]$, with kernel of $H_1(T_K)\to H_1(E_K)$ generated by a unique primitive degree-one class $[\lambda]$ represented by a preferred longitude.

[F5] [[thm-smooth-schoenflies-for-embedded-two-sphere-in-s-three]] under countable choice says every smoothly embedded sphere in $S^3$ separates it into two standard closed balls with common boundary the sphere.

[F6] [[lem-knot-genus-zero-iff-unknot]] under countable choice says that a smooth knot has genus zero exactly when it is ambient isotopic to the standard unknot.

[F7] [[def-knot-connected-sum-and-prime-knot]] fixes the standard-unknot convention.

[F8] [[def-oriented-seifert-surface-and-knot-genus]] defines knot genus as the minimum genus among connected compact oriented spanning surfaces.

[F9] [[lem-torus-curves-same-primitive-class-isotopic-kt18-local]] says every essential simple curve on a torus represents a primitive homology class, and two such curves with classes equal up to sign are isotopic. An annulus in the torus is asserted only for disjoint distinct curves.

## Proof

**Given:** The objects and hypotheses above.

1.1 By [A1], the exact sequence-of-sets choice function in [L1] exists. Let $S\subset\operatorname{int}E_K$ be a smoothly embedded $2$-sphere. By [F5], $S$ bounds two closed $3$-balls in $S^3$. The chosen tube $N(K)$ is connected and disjoint from $S$, so it lies entirely in one component of $S^3\setminus S$. The other closed ball misses $N(K)$ and is therefore contained in $E_K$. This proves the sphere assertion. [A1, L1, F1, F5, given, construct]

1.2 Suppose an embedded disk $D\subset E_K$ has essential boundary $c\subset T_K$. Since $c=\partial D$, its class maps to zero under inclusion $i:T_K\hookrightarrow E_K$. By [F3] and [F4], write $[c]=a[\mu]+b[\lambda]$; then $i_*[c]=a[\mu]$, and $[\mu]$ generates $H_1(E_K;\mathbb Z)$. Hence $a=0$. The curve $c$ is primitive by [F9], so $b=\pm1$ and $[c]=\pm[\lambda]$. [F3, F4, F9, given, algebra]

2.1 Reverse the orientation of $c$ if necessary so that $[c]=[\lambda]$ in step 1.2, and rescale the disk radius in the given product tube to one. In product coordinates $N(K)\cong S^1\times D^2$, the curve $c$ has core degree one by [F2], so its torus class is $(1,k)$ for an integer $k$. The graph $\gamma_k(t)=(e^{it},e^{ikt})$ has that class. By [F9], choose a smooth isotopy $c_s(t)=(u_s(t),z_s(t))$ on the boundary torus from $c$ to $\gamma_k$, stationary near its final time after smooth reparametrization. This isotopy need not trace an embedded annulus on the torus itself. Instead choose a smooth strictly decreasing radius $r(s)$ from $1$ to $1/2$ with nonzero derivative, and use $(s,t)\mapsto(u_s(t),r(s)z_s(t))$ in the tube. Distinct $s$ have distinct radii, while each fixed-$s$ curve is embedded; moreover the time derivative has nonzero radial component and is independent of its circle tangent. This gives a smooth embedded annulus with outer boundary exactly $c$ and inner boundary the radius-$1/2$ copy of $\gamma_k$. Continue it inward by $(r,t)\mapsto(e^{it},r e^{ikt})$ for $0\le r\le1/2$. The stationary final curve makes the collars agree; the inward annulus is smooth and regular even at $r=0$ and has core boundary $K$. The resulting annulus lies otherwise in the interior of $N(K)$. [F1, F2, F3, F9, step 1.2, construct]

3.1 The proper disk $D$ on the exterior side and this annulus on the tube side meet only along $c$. Their union is an embedded disk with boundary $K$; smoothing the corner along $c$ in a product collar leaves that boundary fixed. Thus $K$ bounds a smooth disk, which is a genus-zero Seifert surface. Full AC supplies the countable-choice hypothesis of [F6], so [F6] makes $K$ ambient isotopic to the unknot, contrary to the hypothesis. Therefore no such disk $D$ exists. [L1, F6, F7, F8, given, step 2.1, construct] ∎
